scientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills.\n- Weaknesses: regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone when communicating, makes nasty remarks (from earlier), uncooperative, grumpy (implied). No explicit teamwork strengths.\n\nCandidate C:\n- Strengths: can make correct decisions quickly, puts concern for others above everything, has excellent attention skills.\n- Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education.\n- Also: (maybe other attributes not listed) but we have these.\n\nTeamwork/communication: puts concern for others (positive), excellent attention skills (positive), difficulty communicating ideas (negative), egocentric (negative).\n\nCandidate D:\n- Strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously.\n- Weaknesses: considered moody, regarded as a loner, also earlier mention of arrogant, weak leadership (maybe from later). No explicit teamwork strengths.\n\nNow we need to evaluate for long-distance pilot: reliability, technical competence, calmness, decision-making, teamwork, communication, professional development.\n\nWe have no explicit reliability for any except maybe not. But we have \"puts concern for others above everything\" (C) suggests good crew morale. No explicit reliability for others.\n\nWe have no explicit calmness for any except maybe B handles stress well, C keeps calm in crisis (but that attribute not in original list; but earlier discussion included \"keeps calm in a crisis\" but not in original list. Actually original list didn't include that. So we cannot use it. So we must not mention calmness for C unless it's in data. It's not. So we must stick to data.\n\nThus we have limited info on calmness. B handles stress well (good). A anticipates dangerous situations (good). D responds to unexpected events adequately (good). So all have some.